          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a document refers to the J45 family, is that a code or a category?",
          "answer": "**J45 is a category, not a complete code.** In ICD-10-CM, asthma is the category J45 in Chapter 10 (diseases of the respiratory system). The coding summary says the category is never used at three characters. The extra characters come from two separate axes that are combined (*Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*).\n\n- **Severity axis:** mild intermittent, mild persistent, moderate persistent, severe persistent, and an \"other and unspecified\" option for asthma documented without a severity level.\n- **Episode axis:** uncomplicated, with acute exacerbation, or with status asthmaticus.\n\nEvery combination of the two has its own code, so the J45 family is the category plus these subcodes. The retrieved passages do not list the individual subcode numbers, so I can't give them here. Two named variants, exercise-induced bronchospasm and cough variant asthma, also have their own codes. Occupational asthma from a known agent is classified elsewhere, under lung diseases due to external agents, and the agent is recorded with a separate external-cause code.\n\nWhen a document says \"J45 family,\" it usually means this category and its subcodes. A coder would choose the exact code from the clinical record, not from the category label alone.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
